Point 1: Adequately addressed. The PRP objection is now confronted directly, and the “controlled object” distinction is real: PRP outputs a ranking; PaRC estimates a residual over a fixed anchor. The β-identifiability paragraph is directionally convincing, though it still needs formal notation in Method.

Point 2: Adequately addressed. The rec-specific LLM-ranking neighbors are now present: LLMRank/zero-shot rec rankers and LLM4Rerank are correctly positioned as recommendation reranking/ranking work. RankGPT and PRP remain correctly framed as IR/document-ranking comparators. Plackett-Luce/Elo and Tversky are appropriate additions.

Point 3: Adequately addressed, with caveat. Replacing “BT representation theorem” with scalar-utility stochastic-transitivity limits is the right correction. The text now avoids conflating order sensitivity with cyclic intransitivity. Remaining risk: “no pointwise utility reproduces the order” should be scoped to scalar utility plus monotone pairwise-choice link; otherwise it sounds too universal.

Point 4: Adequately addressed. The validation-floor claim is now correctly limited to validation, and test non-inferiority is framed as empirical within ε, not guaranteed.

Remaining reviewer objection: the β-identifiability claim is still too dense for related work. “Low-rank field,” “α-projection,” and “BT shift/scale” need a precise Method-side definition, or skeptical readers may still see β as post-hoc residualization. Also strip the internal provenance note from any paper-facing draft. Citation spot-checks: [LLMRank](https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.08845), [LLM4Rerank](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12433), [PRP](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.17563), [RankGPT](https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.09542), [Plackett-Luce context](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradley%E2%80%93Terry_model), [stochastic transitivity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stochastic_transitivity).

SCORE_POSITIONING: 8/10
